{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction for criminal conviction or civil liability (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1(o)-(r))","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or found civilly liable for, assault or threats against the landlord's family or employees, theft of property, drug activity, or human trafficking; a three-day notice to quit is required before suit.","key_value":"3-day notice to quit; 2-year filing limit","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental tenants covered by the Eviction Law, including tenants who knowingly permit a person convicted of those offenses to reside on the premises.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Residential rental dwellings covered by the Eviction Law"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may file for eviction, if the tenant has been convicted of or pleaded guilty to, or if a juvenile has been found to be delinquent by the Court due to an offense involving theft of property from the landlord or from tenants residing in the same building or complex.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Evictions from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2(a))","requirement":"A tenant of a landlord-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may be removed only by court order, and only for nonpayment, holding over, disorderly conduct, or willful damage; notice periods range from three days to three months depending on the tenancy.","key_value":"1-month / 3-month / 3-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings, including at-will, year-to-year and month-to-month tenancies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":3,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house evictions (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c))","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, unless the Bureau orders otherwise, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act applies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":2,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"rooming and boarding houses"},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6)","requirement":"A landlord who evicts for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months; a landlord who evicts to board up, demolish or retire a building from residential use may not use it residentially for five years.","key_value":"6 months' owner occupancy; 5-year residential-use bar","coverage_conditions":"Landlords who evicted a tenant for personal occupancy or to permanently board up, demolish or retire the building from residential use.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Treble damages plus attorney fees and costs to the former tenant; civil penalty up to $10,000 per violation; property may not be registered as a planned real estate development for five years.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"When a tenant vacates a dwelling unit after having been given notice that the landlord wishes to personally occupy the unit the landlord must occupy the unit for at least six (6) months.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Reprisal prohibited when tenant exercises civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10)","requirement":"A landlord may not retaliate by eviction, substantial lease alteration, or refusal to renew when a tenant enforces lease or legal rights, files a good-faith health or safety complaint, joins a tenant organization, or refuses retaliatory lease changes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes; owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings are excepted.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"excludes owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings"},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"Tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction requires 90-day notice to quit (N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.)","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; the residential tenant comes with the property. Before evicting a tenant after foreclosure, a buyer who will personally occupy the property must give a 90-day notice to quit.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants in foreclosed property, including month-to-month tenants and owner-occupied two- and three-family units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"foreclosed residential rental property"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69","quoted_span":"Before a tenant can be evicted due to foreclosure, the landlord must provide the tenant with a 90 -day notice to qui t when the foreclosed property has been purchased by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as his or her primary residence.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy","requirement":"Tenants who are at least 62, permanently disabled, or certain disabled veterans and who lived in a building being converted for at least one year before the conversion recording may be protected from eviction for 40 years, subject to income limits.","key_value":"40-year protection; income up to 3 times county average per person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants in buildings converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership who meet the age, disability or veteran criteria and lived there at least one year before the conversion recording; income limits apply.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"condominium, cooperative or fee simple conversion buildings"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever is greater (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.28).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 protected tenancy (Hudson County)","requirement":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 extends protected tenancy to qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible under the 1981 Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act; Hudson County is currently the only qualified county.","key_value":"Hudson County is the only qualified county","coverage_conditions":"Qualified tenants in qualified counties, in buildings converted or being converted, who were not eligible for protected tenancy under the 1981 Act; Hudson County is the only qualified county.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"conversion buildings in qualified counties (Hudson County)"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59","quoted_span":"The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons under the “Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act of 1981” (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 to -61.59; N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.1 to -3.4).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase in excess of rent control ordinance listed as prohibited practice","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent above any applicable rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where the property is not subject to rent control, the landlord may not impose an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental property subject to a rent control or rent leveling ordinance; where no rent control applies, unreasonable or unconscionable increases are prohibited.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"properties subject to rent control or rent leveling ordinances"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"Department of Community Affairs, Division of Housing and Community Resources","quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
